. *------------
. * GENERATE OUTCOME VARIABLE
. 
. * WE COULD BUILD AN INDEX. BASED ON FOUR VARIABLES ON ATTITUDES TOWARD GENDER
>  EQUALITY
. 
. * re-scale variables scaled from 0 (more gender equality) to 4 (less gender e
> quality)
. gen gender_attitude1 = (plh0308_v1 + plh0303) - 2 // substract 3 to start val
> ues at 1
(3,847 missing values generated)

. replace gender_attitude1 = . if plh0308_v1 < 0 | plh0303 < 0 // remove missin
> g data
(26,322 real changes made, 26,322 to missing)

. replace gender_attitude1 = gender_attitude1 / 6 // normalize to 0 to 1
(266 real changes made)

. replace gender_attitude1 = 1- gender_attitude1 // rescale such that a value o
> f 1 means higher gender equality attitudes
(302 real changes made)

.  
. * re-scale variables scaled from 0 (less gender equality) to 4 (more gender e
> quality)
. gen gender_attitude2 = (plh0301 + plh0307) - 2 // substract 2 to start values
>  at 0
(3,847 missing values generated)

. replace gender_attitude2 = . if plh0301 < 0 | plh0307 < 0 // remove missing d
> ata
(26,319 real changes made, 26,319 to missing)

. replace gender_attitude2 = gender_attitude2 / 6 // normalize to 0 to 1
(336 real changes made)

.  
. * merge the variables:
. gen gender_attitude = 1/2 * gender_attitude1 + 1/2 * gender_attitude2 // crea
> te one index variable of gender attitudes
(30,171 missing values generated)

. // gender attitudes are scaled from 0 to 1 with 1 meaning more gender equalit
> y supporting attitudes
